public class ThisKeywordDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee emp = new Employee("Raj", 75000.0);
        emp.display();
    }
}

class Employee {
    String name;
    double salary;

    // Parameter names match instance variable names
    Employee(String name, double salary) {
        // 'this.name' refers to the instance field; 'name' refers to the parameter
        this.name = name;
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Employee: " + this.name + ", Salary: $" + this.salary);
    }
}

pages.content:
The `this` keyword is a reference variable in Java that refers directly to the **current object instance** whose method or constructor is being invoked.

### Common Uses of the `this` Keyword

1. **Disambiguating Shadowed Instance Variables:**
   When a constructor or method parameter shares the identical name with an instance field:
   ```java
   class Laptop {
       String model;
       int ram;

       Laptop(String model, int ram) {
           this.model = model; // this.model is the instance field; model is the parameter
           this.ram = ram;
       }
   }
   ```
   *What happens if you forget `this`?*
   Writing `model = model;` simply assigns the parameter to itself. The instance field `model` remains at its default value (`null`)!

2. **Constructor Chaining using `this()`:*`
   You can invoke another constructor in the same class to avoid duplicating initialization code:
   ```java
   class Laptop {
       String model;
       int ram;

       Laptop(String model) {
           this(model, 16); // Invokes the 2-parameter constructor! Must be the FIRST statement!
       }

       Laptop(String model, int ram) {
           this.model = model;
           this.ram = ram;
       }
   }
   ```

3. **Passing the Current Object as a Parameter:**
   You can pass `this` into external methods or event listeners to register the current instance.

4. **Method Chaining (Fluent APIs):**
   Returning `return this;` from setter methods allows chaining calls:
   ```java
   builder.setTitle("Hello").setColor("Blue").build();
   ```

codeCard.code:
public class ShadowingMistakeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BrokenCar bad = new BrokenCar("Red");
        System.out.println("BrokenCar color: " + bad.color); // null!

        WorkingCar good = new WorkingCar("Red");
        System.out.println("WorkingCar color: " + good.color); // Red!
    }
}

class BrokenCar {
    String color;
    BrokenCar(String color) {
        color = color; // BUG: Assigns parameter to itself! Field stays null.
    }
}

class WorkingCar {
    String color;
    WorkingCar(String color) {
        this.color = color; // CORRECT: Assigns parameter to instance field.
    }
}

